-- **The strength corpus and the three Program Definitions, shipped to
-- production.**
--
-- **No stored number moves.** Nothing here touches a load, a rep, an effective
-- kilo, a 1RM, a training max, a working weight, a Stall Count, a weight or
-- stall history, a set log, or any row an athlete authored. This is additive
-- corpus content: the `Exercise` catalog with its `ExerciseVariant`
-- realizations and its search-only `ExerciseAlias` rows, plus the three
-- `StrengthProgram` definitions with their day shapes and per-lift rule tables.
-- Every athlete's numbers read exactly the same afterwards. The only thing that
-- *changes* on a row that already exists is described under "The refresh", and
-- it is a facet — a movement pattern, a variation group, a variant's display
-- name — never a number.
--
-- ## Why the data is in a migration and not left to the seed
--
-- Production applies `prisma migrate deploy` at boot (`other/litefs.yml`), so
-- the tables arrive on a normal deploy — but nothing runs `prisma db seed`, and
-- nothing may: `prisma/seed.ts` mints the demo user `kody`, fails on a second
-- run, and seeds demo data that must never reach production. Without this file
-- production would have the strength tables and zero rows, and
-- `/training/programs` would render empty.
--
-- So the athlete-independent corpus travels the way Epic Stack's
-- `docs/database.md` prescribes: seeded into a **fresh temporary database** (an
-- empty file, `migrate deploy`, then the two seeders and nothing else), dumped
-- with `sqlite3`, and pasted here as INSERTs.
--
-- The two seeders stay the source of truth and are unchanged —
-- `app/utils/exercise-seed.server.ts` and
-- `app/utils/strength-program-seed.server.ts`. Their "why a seed and not a
-- migration" note still holds for *authoring*: nobody hand-writes these rows.
-- This file is their output for one moment, and the next correction is another
-- dump.
--
-- ## What is dumped, and how that list was determined
--
-- Exactly the tables the two seeders write, read off their code — their
-- `SeedClient` types, plus `buildBlocksCreate` in `app/utils/workout.server.ts`,
-- which is how a day shape's blocks, steps and prescribed sets get written:
--
--   `Exercise`, `ExerciseVariant`, `ExerciseAlias`,
--   `Workout`, `WorkoutBlock`, `WorkoutStep`, `ExerciseSet`,
--   `StrengthProgram`, `StrengthProgramDay`, `StrengthProgramLiftRule`
--
-- Rows: 745 exercises, 753 variants, 56 aliases; 6 day-shape Workouts with 6
-- blocks, 16 steps and 52 prescribed sets; 3 programs, 6 program days, 13 lift
-- rules. Those are the seeders' own returned counts, and a database built from
-- this file alone is byte-identical to the seeded temporary database on all ten
-- tables (timestamps aside).
--
-- **No `CatalogueEntry` row, deliberately.** A day shape is a system-authored
-- `Workout` and not a Catalogue member: its `archetype` is NULL, and
-- `CatalogueEntry`'s three-column foreign key requires a stated archetype. The
-- seeder publishes none, so a `CatalogueEntry` here would put six program days
-- into the Catalogue's browse surface that no seeder ever put there.
--
-- **Nothing athlete-authored is here.** Every `Exercise` row states
-- `authorship: 'system'` with `createdByAthleteId` NULL and every `Workout`
-- states `authorship: 'system'` with `ownerId` NULL — asserted, not assumed:
-- the selects are scoped to the corpus ids and to the `wk_prog_%` day shapes,
-- and the source database had never been touched by an athlete, a session, an
-- import or `prisma/seed.ts`.
--
-- ## The conflict strategy: INSERT OR IGNORE, with a guard where a unique index
-- ## cannot do the job
--
-- Every insert is `INSERT OR IGNORE`. Production already carries the 65
-- `Exercise` rows and their default variants that `20260519130231` and
-- `20260814120000` create, and `OR IGNORE` is the only form that cannot fail a
-- boot-time deploy on *any* unique index — not only the primary key but
-- `ExerciseAlias(exerciseId, text, locale)` and
-- `StrengthProgram(key, variantId)` too. It also cannot clobber a live row: an
-- existing row is left exactly as it is. Foreign key violations are unaffected
-- by `OR IGNORE`, so a genuinely broken reference would still fail loudly.
--
-- `OR IGNORE` alone is **not** enough, and this was caught by applying an
-- earlier draft to a development database that had already been seeded:
--
--   * `WorkoutBlock`, `WorkoutStep` and `ExerciseSet` have no unique key beyond
--     their id, and their ids here are the temporary database's cuids. On a
--     database whose day shapes were already written by the seeder, every block,
--     step and set arrived a second time — six duplicate blocks, and a runner
--     grid with each lift twice.
--   * `StrengthProgramLiftRule`'s unique triple is
--     `(programId, exerciseId, equipment)`. `equipment` used to be NULL on all
--     thirteen rules, and in SQLite a NULL is distinct from every other NULL
--     inside a unique index, so the index did not fire and all thirteen rules
--     duplicated. It now states `'barbell'` (see below), which makes the index
--     real for these rows — the guard stays anyway, because
--     `ExerciseVariant(exerciseId, equipment, angle)` has the same hole with
--     `angle` usually NULL, and a later program that means *this lift, however
--     you realize it* would put a NULL back.
--
-- So those rows carry an explicit `WHERE` guard instead of trusting an index:
--
--   * a variant inserts only if no *other* row already realizes that
--     `(exerciseId, equipment, angle)` with no inventory profile;
--   * a block inserts only if its Workout has no other block — a day shape has
--     exactly one block, so "already shaped" is one condition;
--   * a step inserts only if the block this file wrote exists, and a set only if
--     its step does, so a skipped block skips its whole subtree rather than
--     re-parenting it;
--   * a lift rule inserts only if no other row already holds its
--     `(programId, exerciseId, equipment)` triple, compared with `IS` so a NULL
--     matches a NULL rather than evaluating to unknown.
--
-- ### The refresh
--
-- 38 of the 745 corpus exercises are already in production from
-- `20260519130231`, and those rows predate the movement pattern, the variation
-- group and the variant display names: they hold NULL where the corpus states
-- `squat`, `grp_squat`, `Back Squat (Barbell)`. Ignoring them would ship a
-- database whose picker has no variation grouping and no stated laterality
-- (ADR 0061) on exactly the 38 lifts every athlete uses. So those 38 exercises
-- and their 38 default variants get an explicit `UPDATE` of their facets — the
-- same refresh the seeders' upsert performs, and nothing more.
--
-- The 38 are not a guess: a database with only migrations applied holds exactly
-- 65 `Exercise` rows, and 38 of them share an id with the corpus. Anything else
-- in production under a corpus id would have to be athlete-authored, which is
-- why each `UPDATE` is guarded on `authorship = 'system'` (a variant's guard
-- reads its parent exercise's). An athlete's own movement cannot be reached by
-- these statements — verified against a database where `ex_bb_back_squat` had
-- been taken over as an athlete row: it and its variant came through untouched
-- while the other 744 exercises still landed.
--
-- ## The thirteen lift rules state `equipment = 'barbell'`
--
-- **This is a corrected re-dump, not an edit.** The seeder now resolves the
-- second half of the progression key — the key is the pair
-- `(exerciseId, equipment)` — against the lift's `ExerciseVariant`, and all
-- three programs are barbell programs: they prescribe the barbell lift, name the
-- empty bar as a starting weight, and increment by the smallest pair of plates a
-- bar takes. `PROGRAM_LIFT_EQUIPMENT` in
-- `app/utils/strength-program-seed.server.ts` states it, with the reasoning.
--
-- It was NULL here because the column had never been filled in, and a NULL that
-- silently means *any equipment* is what made StrongLifts 5×5 unrunnable: once
-- logged sets began naming their variant, `'barbell'` on the log stopped
-- matching NULL on the rule, every logged set was dropped and no working weight
-- could ever move. A rule that genuinely means *however you realize it* is still
-- expressible as NULL, and the matcher documents that the leniency is the
-- **rule's** to state — but none of these three mean it.
--
-- **No stored number moves, and this is the one statement worth being careful
-- about.** `equipment` is half of a *key*, not a load: no kilo, rep, working
-- weight, training max or history value differs by a gram. What a stated key
-- does change is which rule a lift matches, so the guarded `UPDATE` below states
-- the same half on any `ProgramLiftState` that a run of these three programs
-- already created with a NULL — otherwise the rule and the athlete's own row
-- would key on different pairs and the run would stop progressing silently. It
-- touches nothing else an athlete owns, and no Load Recompute Notice is owed
-- because no number an athlete has seen is different afterwards.
--
-- ## Re-runnable
--
-- Verified: applied from scratch to an empty database, then applied a second
-- time and a third — counts identical, nothing fails. Applied to a copy of the
-- development database that already held every one of these rows from a live
-- seed run, it neither failed nor duplicated anything.
-- `createdAt`/`updatedAt` are stamped at a fixed 1787011200000
-- (2026-08-18T00:00:00Z) rather than at dump time, so this file is
-- deterministic, and the refresh never rewrites a `createdAt`.

-- Exercise (745 rows)
